  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nNear term through Friday morning...High pressure will result in\nmostly clear skies and light winds. Patchy fog may occur again\ntonight, but it will not be as dense and widespread as it was this\nmorning. \n\nRain/storm chances...Leading chance of storms Friday afternoon\ninto Friday evening appears to carry the highest risk for severe\nthis weekend. Tail of very sharp shortwave seen over Saskatchewan\ntoday swings across MN to Lake Superior Friday afternoon. Cold\nfront reaches western Lake Superior to northwest WI during peak\nheating of the day, with MLCAPEs increasing to 1500-2000J/kg just\nahead of the front. Strongest effective shear is closer to \nshortwave, near 40 kts, with 30+ knots from northern WI to central\nWI by late afternoon. Shear tails off to less than 20 kts farther\neast. A few strong storms with gusty winds the primary hazard are\nexpected over north-central and central WI, shifting east while\nweakening through late evening until around midnight. \n\nBeyond Friday, general idea remains on track. NW flow will bring\nseveral weaker shortwaves across the area, but primary area of\nhigher instability and shear remains west and southwest of\nnortheast WI, Iowa into northern Illinois. Decent agreement that\nafter the front passes later Friday night, Saturday will be dry,\nwith another round of smaller pops late Saturday night. As low\npressure forms over the central Plains late in the weekend, front\nwill return north and bring the highest chances for showers and\nstorms Sunday night into Monday. Some time frame during this\nbroader window will feature widespread showers and embedded\nstorms as the low-level jet and elevated instability will be \naimed toward at least our southern forecast area. This is also \nwhen ECMWF EFI still shows the closest approach of greater signal \nfor higher CAPE/shear, so this will have to be watched especially \ngiven the front will be nearby. Given the front straddling the \narea and PWATs over 1.5 (80-90th percentile), there is a marginal \nrisk (level 1 of 5) for excessive rainfall/flooding especially if \nthere are training storms. \n\nFront settles south of WI next Tuesday, but weaker waves in\nnorthwest flow could still allow for a few showers or storms\nTuesday into Wednesday. Pops for this time remain low, at 15-30%.\n\nTemperatures/smoke...Temperatures will continue to be around normal\nin the low to mid 80s through the weekend as upper flow flattens \nout and weakens. Humidity levels will be comfortable, with \ndewpoints reading in the upper 50s to mid 60s. There will be an\nincrease in dewpoints into the mid and upper 60s on Friday ahead\nof the front. A plume of elevated wildfire smokes works its way \ninto Wisconsin through Friday. Another push of smoke occurs late\nin the weekend into early next week. No significant impacts to \nvisibility or air quality are expected with the smoke.",
